Yes, this is purely a parser problem and can be fixed with a one-line change — no need to touch assignment operations at all.

**Root cause:** In `include/chaiscript/language/chaiscript_parser.hpp`, the `Return()` function (line 2280) calls `Operator()` to parse the return value expression:

```cpp
bool Return() {
    // ...
    if (Keyword("return")) {
        Operator();  // ← only parses arithmetic/logical operators, NOT assignments
        build_match<eval::Return_AST_Node<Tracer>>(prev_stack_top);
        return true;
    }
    // ...
}
```

`Operator()` handles the standard precedence chain (ternary, `||`, `&&`, comparisons, arithmetic, etc.) but **not** assignment operators (`=`, `:=`, `+=`, etc.). Those are handled by `Equation()`, which wraps `Operator()` and adds assignment parsing on top.

So when the parser sees `return this.size = ret["numrows"]`:
1. `Return()` matches `return`
2. `Operator()` parses `this.size` and stops at `=` (not a recognized operator)
3. The return node is built with just `this.size`
4. The remaining `= ret["numrows"]` is left dangling → "Incomplete Block"

**The fix:** Change `Operator()` to `Equation()` on line 2285:

```cpp
if (Keyword("return")) {
    Equation();  // now parses full expressions including assignments
    build_match<eval::Return_AST_Node<Tracer>>(prev_stack_top);
```

This is consistent with how other constructs already work — `If` conditions (line 1938), `for` loop components (line 2075), and function arguments (line 1695) all call `Equation()` rather than `Operator()`. The `Return_AST_Node` eval in `chaiscript_eval.hpp` already handles child expressions correctly, so no changes needed there.

This would enable `return foo = 5`, `return x += 1`, etc., matching C/C++ semantics where assignment is an expression.

**Yes, this is partially possible today, but it requires the script to use try/catch.**

When a registered C++ function throws a `std::runtime_error`, ChaiScript does **not** automatically wrap it in an `eval_error` with script line/column information. The exception propagates straight through the evaluation engine back to the C++ host code. You can see this in `include/chaiscript/language/chaiscript_eval.hpp` — the `Fun_Call_AST_Node::do_eval_internal()` (around line 318) only catches ChaiScript-internal exceptions (`dispatch_error`, `bad_boxed_cast`, `arity_error`, `guard_error`) and wraps those in `eval_error`. A `std::runtime_error` passes through uncaught.

**Workaround using script-side try/catch:**

The script can catch the exception and it will be boxed:

```chaiscript
try {
  var image1 = cv.imread("missing.png", false);
} catch(e) {
  // e contains the runtime_error message, and ChaiScript knows the script location of the try block
}
```

This works because `Try_AST_Node` (line ~1351 in `chaiscript_eval.hpp`) does catch `std::runtime_error` and boxes it for script-level handling.

**What would be needed for automatic wrapping:**

To get script position info on exceptions thrown from C++ functions without a script-side try/catch, a catch for `std::exception` (or `std::runtime_error`) could be added to `Fun_Call_AST_Node::do_eval_internal()` alongside the existing catches, converting it to an `eval_error` with position info. Something like:

```cpp
} catch (const std::exception &e) {
  throw exception::eval_error(
    std::string(e.what()) + " in call to '" + this->children[0]->text + "'");
}
```

This would ensure any C++ exception thrown from a registered function gets wrapped with the call stack trace that `eval_error` provides (the `AST_Node_Impl::eval()` method at line 138 pushes each node onto the `eval_error::call_stack` as it propagates).

So to directly answer: script-side `try/catch` works today, but automatic line/column info on raw C++ exceptions would require a small change to the eval engine.

I investigated this issue. Here's what I found:

**Root Cause: The `fun<Sig>` syntax was documented but never properly implemented.**

The current `fun` implementation in `include/chaiscript/dispatchkit/register_function.hpp:80-83` has only one relevant overload:

```cpp
template<typename T>
Proxy_Function fun(T &&t) { ... }
```

When you write `fun<std::vector<string>(string const&, string const&, bool)>(&ci::split)`, you're explicitly setting `T` to a **function type** `std::vector<string>(string const&, string const&, bool)`, making the parameter type `T&&` a **function rvalue reference**. But `&ci::split` is a **function pointer** — these types don't match. MSVC happens to accept this (likely through a non-standard implicit conversion), while clang correctly rejects it.

For member function pointers (like the example in `chaiscript.hpp:204`), `fun<void (MyClass::*)()>` sets `T` to a pointer-to-member type, which is closer to what `&MyClass::method` produces, so that form may work more broadly. But for free functions, specifying a function type vs. a function pointer type is a real mismatch.

There's already a `\todo` acknowledging this at `unittests/compiled_tests.cpp:536`:
```cpp
/// \todo fix overload resolution for fun<>
```

And the test code itself uses the `static_cast` workaround (`compiled_tests.cpp:544-546`).

**To fix this**, an additional overload of `fun` could be added that accepts a function pointer when the signature is explicitly specified:

```cpp
// For free functions: fun<Ret(Args...)>(&overloaded)
template<typename Ret, typename... Args>
Proxy_Function fun(Ret (*f)(Args...)) {
  return dispatch::detail::make_callable(f, dispatch::detail::function_signature(f));
}

// For member functions: fun<Ret(Class::*)(Args...)>(&Class::overloaded)
template<typename Ret, typename Class, typename... Args>
Proxy_Function fun(Ret (Class::*f)(Args...)) {
  return dispatch::detail::make_callable(f, dispatch::detail::function_signature(f));
}
// (plus const/volatile/noexcept variants as needed)
```

These overloads would let the user write `fun<Ret(Params...)>(&overloaded)` and have the explicit template arguments guide overload resolution of the function pointer argument, which is the intended use case. The existing `template<typename T> fun(T&&)` overload would continue to handle the non-ambiguous (single overload) case via deduction.

Alternatively, a simpler single overload using `std::type_identity_t` could prevent deduction on T, forcing the user to specify it explicitly — though this might interfere with the deduction-based path.

**TL;DR**: This is a known issue (see the TODO). The cheatsheet's "preferred" syntax for free function overloads doesn't work on standards-conforming compilers. The `static_cast` approach is the correct workaround for now. A fix would require adding explicit overloads of `fun` for function pointers and member function pointers. Would you like me to implement a fix?

Great question. I dug into the internals to understand how this could work cleanly.

### How member attributes work today

When you register `chaiscript::fun(&MyClass::x)`, the system creates an `Attribute_Access<T, Class>` proxy (`include/chaiscript/dispatchkit/proxy_functions.hpp:600-664`). Its `do_call` checks whether the *object* is const:

- **Non-const object** → returns a mutable `std::ref(o->*m_attr)` (writable from script)
- **Const object** → returns a `std::cref(o->*m_attr)` (read-only from script)

So if you pass a `const` object into ChaiScript, its attributes are already read-only. But for non-const objects, all registered attributes are writable.

### Suggested approach: `Const_Attribute_Access` + `chaiscript::fun_readonly()`

The cleanest approach I see is:

**1. Add a `Const_Attribute_Access` class** in `proxy_functions.hpp` alongside `Attribute_Access`. It would be nearly identical but always takes the const path in `do_call`:

```cpp
template<typename T, typename Class>
class Const_Attribute_Access final : public Proxy_Function_Base {
public:
  explicit Const_Attribute_Access(T Class::*t_attr)
      : Proxy_Function_Base(param_types(), 1)
      , m_attr(t_attr) {
  }

  bool is_attribute_function() const noexcept override { return true; }

  // ... operator== and call_match same as Attribute_Access ...

protected:
  Boxed_Value do_call(const Function_Params &params, const Type_Conversions_State &t_conversions) const override {
    const Boxed_Value &bv = params[0];
    // Always use the const path, regardless of object constness
    const Class *o = bv.is_const()
        ? boxed_cast<const Class *>(bv, &t_conversions)
        : static_cast<const Class *>(boxed_cast<Class *>(bv, &t_conversions));
    return do_call_impl(o);
  }

private:
  auto do_call_impl(const Class *o) const {
    if constexpr (std::is_pointer<T>::value) {
      return detail::Handle_Return<const T>::handle(o->*m_attr);
    } else {
      return detail::Handle_Return<typename std::add_lvalue_reference<typename std::add_const<T>::type>::type>::handle(o->*m_attr);
    }
  }

  static std::vector<Type_Info> param_types() { return {user_type<T>(), user_type<Class>()}; }
  T Class::*m_attr;
};
```

**2. Add a `fun_readonly()` convenience function** in `register_function.hpp`:

```cpp
/// Creates a read-only attribute accessor from a member data pointer.
/// The attribute will not be assignable from ChaiScript even on non-const objects.
template<typename T, typename Class>
Proxy_Function fun_readonly(T Class::*member) {
  return Proxy_Function(
      chaiscript::make_shared<dispatch::Proxy_Function_Base, 
          dispatch::Const_Attribute_Access<T, Class>>(member));
}
```

**3. Usage would be:**

```cpp
struct MyClass {
  int x;
  std::string name;
};

chaiscript::ChaiScript chai;
chai.add(chaiscript::user_type<MyClass>(), "MyClass");
chai.add(chaiscript::fun_readonly(&MyClass::x), "x");       // read-only
chai.add(chaiscript::fun_readonly(&MyClass::name), "name");  // read-only
chai.add(chaiscript::fun(&MyClass::some_method), "some_method"); // methods still work
```

From ChaiScript, `obj.x` would return the value but `obj.x = 42` would fail because the returned `Boxed_Value` holds a `const` reference.

### Why this approach

- **Mirrors existing patterns** — `Const_Attribute_Access` is a natural sibling to `Attribute_Access`, following the same structure
- **Minimal surface area** — one new class + one new free function; no changes to the dispatch engine, `Boxed_Value`, or evaluation
- **No runtime overhead** — the constness is baked in at registration time, not checked dynamically
- **Works with the existing const system** — `Handle_Return<const T&>` already produces const `Boxed_Value`s via `std::cref`, and the assignment operator in the eval engine already rejects writes to const values

### Alternative: template bool on `Attribute_Access`

Instead of a separate class, you could add a `bool ReadOnly` template parameter to `Attribute_Access` and use `if constexpr` in `do_call`. This avoids code duplication but makes the existing class slightly more complex. Either way works — I'd lean toward the separate class for clarity since the two classes are small.

