This is a great question. In ChaiScript, methods defined inside a `class` block are not stored on the `Dynamic_Object` itself — they're registered as functions in the global dispatch engine, guarded by the object's type name. That's why `get_attrs()` only shows member variables.

There are a couple of ways to call these methods from C++:

### Option 1: Use `eval()` with the object in scope (simplest)

You can put the object back into ChaiScript's scope using `add_global` or `set_global`, then call the method via `eval`:

```cpp
chaiscript::ChaiScript chai;

// Define the class
chai.eval(R"(
  class Foo {
    def Foo() {}
    def Bar(x) { return x + 5; }
  }
)");

// Create the object
auto obj = chai.eval("Foo()");

// Add it to ChaiScript's global scope
chai.add_global(obj, "my_foo");

// Call the method
auto result = chai.eval<int>("my_foo.Bar(3)");
std::cout << result << std::endl; // prints 8
```

### Option 2: Eval a lambda to get a callable `std::function`

If you want a reusable C++ callable, you can eval a lambda that wraps the method call:

```cpp
auto obj = chai.eval("Foo()");
chai.add_global(obj, "my_foo");

auto bar = chai.eval<std::function<int(int)>>("fun(x) { my_foo.Bar(x) }");
std::cout << bar(3) << std::endl; // prints 8
```

### Option 3: Pass the object as a parameter (similar to the workaround above)

This avoids polluting global scope:

```cpp
auto obj = chai.eval("Foo()");

// Create a dispatch function that takes the object as a parameter
auto caller = chai.eval<std::function<Boxed_Value(Boxed_Value, int)>>(
    "fun(o, x) { o.Bar(x) }"
);
```

### Why there's no direct C++ method lookup

The architecture stores class methods as globally dispatched functions (wrapped in `Dynamic_Object_Function` — see `include/chaiscript/dispatchkit/dynamic_object_detail.hpp`) rather than as per-object method tables. The dispatch engine matches calls based on the object's type name at runtime. This means there isn't a simple `obj.get_method("Bar")` API — the method resolution always goes through the dispatch engine.

The `eval`-based approaches above are the intended way to interact with ChaiScript-defined objects from C++. Option 1 is the most straightforward for most use cases.

Great question! Here's how to expose `system` (or better alternatives) to ChaiScript.

### Basic: Exposing `std::system`

```cpp
#include <chaiscript/chaiscript.hpp>
#include <cstdlib>

int main() {
  chaiscript::ChaiScript chai;
  chai.add(chaiscript::fun([](const std::string &cmd) {
    return std::system(cmd.c_str());
  }), "system");

  chai.eval(R"(
    system("echo hello")
  )");
}
```

This works but **cannot capture output** — `std::system` only returns the exit code.

### Capturing Output with `popen`

For output capture without any external dependencies:

```cpp
#include <chaiscript/chaiscript.hpp>
#include <cstdio>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <array>

std::string exec(const std::string &cmd) {
  std::array<char, 256> buffer;
  std::string result;
  FILE *pipe = popen(cmd.c_str(), "r");
  if (!pipe) { throw std::runtime_error("popen() failed"); }
  while (fgets(buffer.data(), buffer.size(), pipe)) {
    result += buffer.data();
  }
  int status = pclose(pipe);
  if (status != 0) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Command failed with status " + std::to_string(status));
  }
  return result;
}

int main() {
  chaiscript::ChaiScript chai;
  chai.add(chaiscript::fun(&exec), "exec");
  chai.add(chaiscript::fun([](const std::string &cmd) {
    return std::system(cmd.c_str());
  }), "system");

  chai.eval(R"(
    var output = exec("echo hello world")
    print(output)

    // or just fire-and-forget:
    system("notepad.exe")
  )");
}
```

`popen`/`pclose` are available on both Windows (as `_popen`/`_pclose`) and POSIX. For portable code:

```cpp
#ifdef _WIN32
  #define popen _popen
  #define pclose _pclose
#endif
```

### Libraries for Richer Process Control

If you need more than just "run and capture stdout" (e.g., stdin piping, stderr capture, async execution, timeouts):

- **[reproc](https://github.com/DaanDeMeyer/reproc)** — Lightweight, cross-platform, C/C++ API. Handles stdin/stdout/stderr redirection cleanly. Good fit for embedding.
- **[Boost.Process](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/release/doc/html/process.html)** (`boost/process.hpp`) — Full-featured if you already use Boost. Supports async I/O via Boost.Asio.
- **[subprocess.h](https://github.com/sheredom/subprocess.h)** — Single-header C library, very easy to integrate.

For most use cases, the `popen` approach above is sufficient and has zero dependencies.

